00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:57.040
And I know that
our customers will

00:12:57.040 --> 00:12:59.800
be chomping at the bit,
won't be able to wait

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:01.150
to get this functionality.

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:02.830
They're going to be
probably as excited

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:04.740
as we were to work on this.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.950
And I want to give a shout out
to Steve Holm and Ben and Eric,

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:08.950
who have worked on this.

00:13:08.950 --> 00:13:11.170
And a huge thank you
to Thijs and his team

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:13.000
for making this tool
available, which

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.400
will make our users'
lives so much easier.

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:18.725
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:23.550
THIJS VAN AS: Can we
switch back to the slides?

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:25.530
Thank you very much,
Ursula, and also thank you

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:31.110
for being flexible and doing
the demo on a different device.

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:34.740
Great, so when we
think about what

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:37.890
was the reason why we
introduced Gmail add-ons,

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:41.640
it's all about making our
users more productive,

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:44.070
helping them waste
less time in Gmail.

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:47.130
And we did this by bringing
you, by bringing them

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:50.990
your app's functionality
close to where they need it.

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:57.360
And we do this by showing
those add-on icons in the UI

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:59.760
for each message
where it's relevant.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:01.920
Now, there's, of
course, a risk here,

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:04.080
because this might
result in like a wildfire

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:06.990
of different icons
for different add-ons.

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:10.320
So what we did is we built
the framework in such a way

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:12.210
that you can trigger
your add-on when

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:13.960
it's contextually relevant.

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:15.570
So for example,
the Google Calendar

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:17.610
add-on that you
just saw, it's only

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:21.000
triggered for an
email that contained

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:23.692
an intent from the sender
to set up a meeting.

00:14:23.692 --> 00:14:25.650
It doesn't just trigger
if the meeting contains

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:27.110
a data or a time mentioned.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:29.910
There's actually
an intent behind it

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:32.520
when the user wants to,
when the sender of the email

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:34.260
wants to meet.

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:37.740
Now, I want to reiterate that
it was very important for us

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:39.930
to make this cross-platform,
because we realized

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:42.930
there were some options today
to extend Gmail on the web,

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:44.970
but given the rise
of mobile usage,

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:48.360
we wanted to make sure this
was desktop and mobile.

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:52.350
Now with that, we realized
there was not a good development

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:56.160
pattern today to do this
on [INAUDIBLE] mobile,

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:58.410
and especially not
doing it efficiently

00:14:58.410 --> 00:14:59.850
without a lot of hassle.

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:02.790
So we started thinking about
a new development paradigm

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:05.160
for these Gmail add-ons.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:08.820
And first of all, Gmail add-ons,
they're written in Apps Script.

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:10.500
If you're not familiar
with App Script,

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:12.180
Apps Script is a
JavaScript runtime

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:17.460
in the cloud specifically built
for integrating and enhancing

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:19.020
G Suite applications.

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:21.750
So with tons of APIs to
integrate with other G Suite

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:25.074
apps, like it's very easy to
build like a quick integration

00:15:25.074 --> 00:15:27.240
with Google Drive, Google
Calendar, and Google Docs,

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:29.410
for example.

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:32.880
And it's also super easy
to hook up your own APIs

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.650
into Apps Script.

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:38.220
You could already built Google
Docs add-ons with Apps Scripts,

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:41.460
but now today you can
also build Gmail add-ons.

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.200
However, this doesn't
say anything yet

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:45.630
about how does this
cross-platform.

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:49.950
And for that, we built a new
design system using cards.

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:53.707
So let me walk through
that with an example.

00:15:53.707 --> 00:15:56.040
So what you're looking at
here is like an example add-on

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:57.210
that we called Contacts.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:59.610
And what it does, it
shows for all the people

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:01.530
in an email conversation,
it will show me

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:05.370
some contact information
coming from my address book.

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:08.880
And by means of this
add-on, I'm going

00:16:08.880 --> 00:16:13.990
to tell you what the card
system actually means.

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:17.160
So in this case, all the
information for one contact

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:18.420
is all on one card.

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:22.630
Now a card consists of a couple
of individual components.

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:24.030
So there's a header of the card.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:26.340
In this case, the header
contains the profile picture

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:29.564
of the sender, and it contains
the name of the contact.

00:16:29.564 --> 00:16:31.230
And there's a couple
of other variations

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:33.600
that we have, but
here's an example.

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:36.210
Then a card contains
one or more sections.

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:38.280
And in these sections,
you basically

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:40.590
combine your
different UI elements.

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:43.920
And for these UI elements,
we call them widgets here.

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:46.410
Here's an example of
a widget, so where

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:49.830
we show the email
address of the sender.

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:51.390
Currently, we have
a whole library

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:54.120
available of UI
widgets, anything

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:56.410
from interactive widgets
like text inputs,

00:16:56.410 --> 00:16:59.820
dropdowns, checkboxes to
information representation

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:02.520
widgets, like
images, paragraphs,

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:06.000
and label description
[INAUDIBLE].

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:10.079
And sometimes your add-on, it
needs to return multiple cards

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:11.140
to the user.

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:12.960
So in the case of
this Contacts app,

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:15.660
we might want to show
a card for each person

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:17.579
in the conversation,
also the people that

00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:19.359
are cc'd, for example.

00:17:19.359 --> 00:17:21.682
And in that case, what
our system will do

00:17:21.682 --> 00:17:23.640
is it will collapse the
multiple cards that you

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:25.500
have into one overview.

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:27.960
And then the user can
tap on an individual card

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:31.461
to expand it and
basically do a deep dive.

00:17:31.461 --> 00:17:33.210
And that's, for example,
what you saw just

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:37.740
in the ProsperWorks
add-on just a moment ago.

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:40.296
All right, so that's
the card system.

00:17:40.296 --> 00:17:41.670
But it's still
pretty high level.

00:17:41.670 --> 00:17:43.860
So how does this look in code?

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:46.290
I want to show you
that by means of a very

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:48.300
tiny, simplified example.

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:51.960
So I made the Contacts
example even simpler.

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:54.580
So super tiny card
with just a header

00:17:54.580 --> 00:17:57.180
with one section that
contains just the email

00:17:57.180 --> 00:18:01.210
address of the sender and
the section as a header.

00:18:01.210 --> 00:18:02.960
So let's look at how
this looks in code.

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:06.040
As a first step, we're creating
a new instance of a card

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:08.730
by calling the
createCard method.

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:11.520
Now we're giving this card
a header, because every card

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:12.690
needs a header.

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:16.270
And in that header, we're giving
it two pieces of information.

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:19.650
In this case, the profile
picture of the sender and also

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:21.690
the name of the sender.

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:23.614
And as I mentioned
before, there's

00:18:23.614 --> 00:18:24.780
a bunch of other variations.

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:26.029
You can leave out the picture.

00:18:26.029 --> 00:18:30.000
You can add some other lines of
text, but this is basically it.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.850
Now we're adding a
section to this card.

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:36.180
And in that section, we can use
it to put UI widgets in there.

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:40.330
But first, before
adding UI widgets,

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:43.470
we want to give this
section a title or a header.

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:44.890
That's optional,
but in this case,

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:47.114
we're calling it Contact Info.

00:18:47.114 --> 00:18:48.780
And now let's create
our one and only UI

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:52.020
widget, which in this
case, we use the key value

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:53.910
widgets, which is
pretty generic.

00:18:53.910 --> 00:18:57.330
It's a way to create [? pairs ?]
of a label and a description.

00:18:57.330 --> 00:18:59.070
In this case,
we're using an icon

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:02.670
as a label, the email icon, and
we're using the email address

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:04.440
as the description.

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:06.420
But there's a bunch
of other variations.

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:08.490
It's quite flexible.

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:11.760
So as you see here, this is
quite a short piece of code,

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:14.460
of course, also quite
a simple add-on.

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:17.460
But it's really simple,
and the magic here

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:21.120
is this translates
into a nice UI,

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:25.920
fully material design, not just
on one platform, but on three.

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:30.620
And you've already seen
the demo on a Chrome device

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:32.080
and on an iPhone.

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:34.920
And hopefully the next demo we
will do on an Android device.

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:36.960
And with that, I'd
like to introduce

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:39.180
Krzysztof Oblucki,
who is a software

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:40.620
engineer at Salesforce.

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:42.390
And he will show
you a demo that he

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:44.230
built for a Salesforce add-on.

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:52.549
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:53.324 --> 00:19:55.490
KRZYSZTOF OBLUCKI: Hi, my
name is Krzysztof Oblucki.

00:19:55.490 --> 00:19:57.590
I'm an engineer at
Salesforce, and I'm

00:19:57.590 --> 00:20:01.220
going to show you the stuff
that we've built with Google

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:02.810
in the past couple of weeks.

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:05.550
It's pretty exciting.

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:09.440
I'm going to go into my
Gmail, switch to my account

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:13.100
here, awesome.

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:16.640
And essentially, our use case
is very similar to ProsperWorks.

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:19.760
We want to make sure that
our salespeople and sales

00:20:19.760 --> 00:20:24.800
guys have a much easier time
getting information queried out

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:27.890
